# Copy authoring

Apply whenever writing or editing website copy, metadata, accessibility text or repository prose.
Use British English. Preserve technical accuracy, approved claims and the user's explicit wording.
These are house-style requirements, not a test of whether a human or AI wrote something.

## Prohibited punctuation and patterns

- Never use em dashes (Unicode U+2014), the HTML entities `&mdash;`, `&#8212;` or
  `&#x2014;`, or an escape that renders the same character in authored copy.
  Use a comma, full stop, colon or parentheses according to meaning. Do not substitute doubled
  hyphens or a parenthetical en dash to imitate an em dash. Keep ordinary compound-word hyphens.
- Do not use formulaic contrast as persuasion: “not just X, but Y”, “not only X but also Y”,
  “this isn't about X, it's about Y”, or “X, not Y”. State the actual service or benefit directly.
  Necessary factual distinctions and instructions remain valid.
- Do not use self-answered rhetorical fragments such as “The result? ...” or “Why? Because ...”.
  Write a complete statement instead. Genuine questions in forms and FAQs remain valid.
- Do not add generic scene-setting such as “in today's rapidly evolving landscape”,
  “in an increasingly digital world”, or “now more than ever”. Start with the subject.
- Do not use canned signposts such as “it's worth noting”, “importantly”, “in conclusion”,
  “in summary”, or “the bottom line” to pad paragraphs or repeat a section's message.
- Do not use decorative metaphors or inflated filler such as “delve into”, “a rich tapestry”,
  “a testament to”, “unlock potential”, “harness the power”, or “embark on a journey”.
- Do not use vague promotional labels such as “game-changing”, “revolutionary”, “cutting-edge”,
  “seamless”, “world-class”, or “best-in-class” in place of a specific, supported explanation.
- Do not use “leverage”, “utilise”, “foster”, “underscore” or “pivotal” as ornamental substitutes
  for direct words such as use, help, show or important. A literal technical meaning is valid.
- Do not build repetitive paragraphs from identical openings, stacked transitions or repeated
  three-part slogans. Use the structure the information needs. Meaningful lists of three items,
  parallel navigation labels and established technical terms remain valid.
- Do not add decorative emoji, excessive bold emphasis, invented quotations, or unsupported
  superlatives, figures, client references or credentials.

User-supplied or explicitly approved wording takes precedence over vocabulary and rhetorical
preferences. Do not silently rewrite it to apply these preferences. The em-dash prohibition still
applies unless the user explicitly asks to preserve that punctuation. Do not alter code identifiers,
URLs, external quotations or data to enforce prose preferences; explain a material conflict.

## Required correction workflow

1. Inspect copy in the files involved in the task and shared copy they render. Before finishing,
   search relevant source for U+2014 and encoded equivalents, and read the text for the patterns above.
2. When a violation is found in existing authored copy, make the smallest grammatical correction
   that removes it. Preserve meaning, factual distinctions, layout, links and approved terminology.
   Do not rewrite whole sections or expand claims. Correct clear incidental violations in touched
   copy; do not turn a scoped task into an unrelated site-wide rewrite.
3. Read the revised sentence in context, format modified files and run the appropriate checks.
4. Briefly report corrections to existing copy and where they were made. Do not ask for separate
   permission for a minimal correction already required by this rule. Flag genuine ambiguity.

## Research and interpretation

Reviewed 9 November 2026. Research reports tendencies across corpora; individual words and
punctuation do not establish authorship. The specific prohibitions above are ModusFi's editorial
choices. They combine the user's preferences with research and plain-language guidance, rather
than attributing every prohibition to a source.

- [GOV.UK: Use clear language](https://guidance.publishing.service.gov.uk/writing-to-gov-uk-standards/writing-guidelines/clear-language/):
  plain English, active voice, specific language and avoiding empty jargon.
- [Microsoft: Use simple words, concise sentences](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/style-guide/word-choice/use-simple-words-concise-sentences):
  precise words, consistent terms and removal of unnecessary wording and modifiers.
- [Why Does ChatGPT “Delve” So Much? (COLING 2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.11385):
  lexical overrepresentation in model output, including delve and underscore.
- [Do LLMs write like humans? (PNAS)](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2422455122):
  grammatical and rhetorical variation across human and model writing and across genres.
